## 3. Codebase Analysis & Strategic Guidance

The following analysis is based on my direct review of the current codebase. Use these notes and tips to guide your implementation.

### Relevant Existing Code
*   **File:** `pom.xml`
    *   **Summary:** Parent aggregator defining Quarkus BOM imports, shared dependency versions (Stripe, AWS SDK, Testcontainers), pluginManagement for Quarkus/Spotless/Jacoco/Surefire, and a single `modules/core-platform` child with only a `native` profile currently defined.
    *   **Recommendation:** Extend this file to capture all Quarkus build profiles (`dev`, `test`, `prod`, and `native`) with the required `quarkus.profile` activation, ensure Spotless/Jacoco goals run in the default lifecycle (`mvn clean verify`), and document extension sets so the README/test-strategy reference can cite this configuration.
*   **File:** `modules/core-platform/pom.xml`
    *   **Summary:** The concrete Quarkus module currently aggregates most dependencies (ARC, cache, container-image-jib, Flyway, Panache, JDBC, Kubernetes, Mailer, Micrometer, OpenTelemetry, Qute, REST, Jackson, Scheduler, SmallRye Health/JWT/OpenAPI, Vert.x HTTP, Thumbnailator, MapStruct, bcrypt, AWS SDKs, Quinoa deployment, and required test stacks).
    *   **Recommendation:** Cross-check this dependency list against `docs/java-project-standards.adoc` and the Standard Kit to confirm RESTEasy Reactive (use `quarkus-rest` or switch to `quarkus-resteasy-reactive` explicitly), Panache, Scheduler, AWS S3, Stripe, and Spotless/Jacoco coverage expectations are satisfied; add any missing Quarkus extensions or managed versions via the parent POM so downstream modules inherit the same bill of materials.
*   **File:** `docs/java-project-standards.adoc`
    *   **Summary:** Canonical engineering standards specifying Maven structure, Java 21, forbidden dependencies, mandatory Quarkus extensions (ARC, RESTEasy Reactive, REST Jackson, Panache, JDBC PostgreSQL, SmallRye Health, Micrometer Prometheus), observability requirements, and test stack expectations (Quarkus JUnit5, RestAssured, JaCoCo, OpenTelemetry, etc.).
    *   **Recommendation:** Use this document as the checklist while editing the POM—explicitly call out in commit/test-strategy text which sections you have satisfied (e.g., required dependencies, coverage gates) and ensure no prohibited dependencies (like Lombok) sneak into the module tree.
*   **File:** `README.md`
    *   **Summary:** High-level product overview, CI description, setup instructions, and documentation index; currently lacks an explicit Maven module inventory or reference to coverage expectations beyond the CI badge.
    *   **Recommendation:** Add a concise subsection (likely under "Technology Stack" or "Continuous Integration & Quality Gates") enumerating existing Maven modules/profiles and linking to the new test strategy so future contributors know how `core-platform`, admin SPA, etc., map onto the build.

### Implementation Tips & Notes
*   **Tip:** `docs/quality/test_strategy.md` does not exist yet—create it alongside the Maven work to capture coverage thresholds, module inventory, and the profile/extension list demanded by the acceptance criteria. Cite both `pom.xml` updates and the standards document inside the new Markdown file.
*   **Tip:** When defining `%dev/%test/%prod` Maven/Quarkus profiles, prefer reusable property blocks (e.g., `quarkus.profile.dev`) so the parent POM enforces consistent flags for all child modules; describe these settings in the README/test-strategy deliverables.
*   **Note:** The parent POM already wires Spotless and JaCoCo via pluginManagement but does not automatically execute them—ensure your changes either bind the appropriate goals to default phases or document the `mvn clean verify` command path that triggers them so the acceptance test "runs locally" is satisfied.
*   **Warning:** Updating dependencies or adding new Quarkus extensions inside `modules/core-platform/pom.xml` can affect native-image builds; keep the `quarkus-bom` alignment intact and rerun `./mvnw verify -Pnative` locally (or at least document that it passes) to avoid breaking the GraalVM profile mandated by the Standard Kit.
